    {q:"What makes El Valle de Antón unique?", a:"It's one of the largest inhabited volcano craters in the Americas. The geography creates a distinctive climate — permanently green, cooler than the surrounding lowlands, and sheltered. The combination of hot springs, a Sunday market, and beautiful hiking trails within the town itself makes for an unusually active lifestyle."},
    {q:"Is El Valle de Antón right for full-time expat living?", a:"It suits a specific profile: retirees who want nature and a slow pace, have access to good income (costs are high), and don't mind the relative isolation. It works less well for families with children (limited schooling options) or remote workers who need reliable fast internet. Many residents maintain a second home in the capital."},
    {q:"How does El Valle compare to Boquete?", a:"Both are highland towns with cool climates and established expat communities. El Valle is closer to Panama City (2 hours vs 6) and has a more resort-like, upscale feel. Boquete has a larger, more active full-time expat community, better internet, and a broader service ecosystem. El Valle tends to attract more part-time residents and second-home buyers."},
    {q:"What are El Valle's hot springs like?", a:"Several natural thermal pools and spas operate in the valley — some rustic, some more developed. They range from free public access to private spa facilities. The thermal water is a genuine draw and a regular part of local life for both residents and weekend visitors."},
